<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.5.7: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01656</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.5.7: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>c8de7da9-169f-49c6-81f4-7e022a7a0fa3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>To help students’ understanding of key points from the lesson, discuss each of the following ways to factor trinomials:</para>
<list>
  <item> To factor a trinomial of the form \(x^2 + bx + c\), undo FOIL to find the binomial factors: </item>
  <list>
    <item> Write the factors as two binomials with the first terms \(x\). </item>
    <item> Find two numbers that multiply to \(c\) and add to \(b\). </item>
    <item> Use these numbers as the last terms of the binomial factors. </item>
    <item> Check by multiplying the factors together. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> To factor a trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\), use the trial and error method: </item>
  <list>
    <item> Factor any GCF. </item>
    <item> Find all the factor pairs of the first term. </item>
    <item> Find all the factor pairs of the third term.</item>
    <item> Test all the possible combinations of the factors until the correct product is found. </item>
    <item> Check by multiplying the factors together. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> For another way to factor a trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\), use the “\(ac\)” method: </item>
  <list>
    <item> This method works on any trinomial of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\). </item>
    <item> Factor any GCF. </item>
    <item> Find the product \(ac\). </item>
    <item> Find two numbers, \(m\) and \(n\), that multiply to \(ac\) and add to \(b\). </item>
    <item> Split the middle term using \(m\) and \(n\): \(ax^2 + mx + nx + c\).</item>
    <item> Factor by grouping. </item>
    <item> Check by multiplying the factors together. </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list>
  <item> To factor a polynomial that does not appear to be written in the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\), use substitution: </item>
  <list>
    <item> Factor any GCF. </item>
    <item> Substitute \(u\) for a common expression in the first terms. </item>
    <item> Factor as if the trinomial is of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c\). </item>
    <item> Replace \(u\) with the original value. </item>
  </list>
</list></section>
</content>
